                <h2>Pre-Launch: 8 Essential Checks Before Starting Ads</h2>
<p>Before officially launching ad campaigns, complete the following inspections. Hold off on launching if any item fails:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Account qualification completeness</strong>: Business license, legal representative info, address proof – all valid and current</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Promoted product/service registered</strong>: Industries like finance, healthcare, and gaming require advance资质证明 preparation</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Landing page compliance review complete</strong>: Privacy policy, refund terms, contact info present and accessible</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Target market policies confirmed</strong>: Middle East, Europe, etc. have special requirements for religious content and data privacy</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Ad creatives passed internal review</strong>: Materials featuring person portraits, copyrighted music, sensitive elements need separate annotation</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Pixel/SDK installation compliance</strong>: User data collection must comply with local GDPR or CCPA requirements</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Payment channel and landing page consistency</strong>: Payee info must match the advertiser's entity</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Timezone and budget scheduling calibrated</strong>: Avoid timezone errors causing overnight overspending or budget waste</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: A utility app team once ignored age restriction classifications for their target market during their first overseas launch, resulting in forced takedown of their ad creatives. Re-review took 3 business days.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Active Monitoring: 6 Key Metrics for Weekly Routine Inspections</h2>
<p>After launching, establish a weekly compliance inspection routine focusing on these metrics:</p>
<h3>1. Account Health Check</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Login IP stability (frequent IP changes may trigger security alerts)</li>
<li>[ ] Account balance adequacy to avoid service interruption from insufficient funds</li>
<li>[ ] BM (Business Manager) permission allocation compliance – removed accounts for departed employees</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Campaign Data Anomaly Monitoring</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Daily spend within preset thresholds (150% warning line recommended)</li>
<li>[ ] CTR (click-through rate) abnormal fluctuations (sudden spikes or drops)</li>
<li>[ ] Conversion rate deviation from industry benchmarks</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Creative &amp; Copy Compliance Spot-Check</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Platform-prohibited absolute terms used (e.g., "best," "#1")</li>
<li>[ ] Promoted content consistent with registered business scope at account setup</li>
<li>[ ] Click-bait behaviors present (fake countdowns, exaggerated offers)</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Policy Update Attention</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Platform policy center updates affecting your industry</li>
<li>[ ] New regulations in target markets (e.g., recent EU Digital Services Act restrictions on ad tracking)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Anomaly Response: Quick Steps for Account Bans/Creative Rejections</h2>
<p>When receiving platform violation notices, handle as follows:</p>
<h3>Step 1: Locate the Issue (0-2 hours)</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Check specific violation reasons in ad backend – view details, not just overviews</li>
<li>[ ] Distinguish between account-level vs. ad/creative-level violations</li>
<li>[ ] Screenshot the violation notice (may be needed for appeals)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Initial Assessment (2-4 hours)</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Determine if violation is "appealable" (forged materials, IP infringement have low success rates)</li>
<li>[ ] Check if similar ads have the same issues to prevent secondary violations</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm spend amount on violating ads to assess loss scope</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 3: Execute Appeal or Rectification</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Choose appeal</strong>: If judged as misjudgment, submit appeal through official channels with supporting documents</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Choose rectification</strong>: Modify creative or adjust targeting, resubmit for review</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Backup plan</strong>: If other accounts are active, consider temporarily migrating budget to avoid business interruption</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 4: Review and Prevention (within 48 hours)</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Log violation type, trigger reason, handling duration to internal knowledge base</li>
<li>[ ] Update creative review checklist with newly discovered violation scenarios</li>
<li>[ ] If policy misunderstanding, organize team study of latest platform policy documents</li>
</ul>
<h2>Conclusion: Compliance is the Lowest-Cost Long-Term Investment</h2>
<p>Many operations teams only prioritize compliance after problems occur, overlooking the value of inspection mechanisms. Instead of spending significant time on appeals, invest 30 minutes in the checklist above at the start of each campaign cycle. Recommend converting these checklist items into spreadsheets, assigning different team members for regular completion, enabling visual compliance inspection management.</p>
